  "query_preview": "model trends along with upstream radar. A few\nthunderstorms can't be ruled out, but little lightning is being\nobserved upstream with little change to the environment\nexpected. However, there could be some localized convective \ndownpours that reduce visibility...this chance is currently too \nlow to include in the TAF. As the front slides through toward\ndaybreak, winds will shift more northerly and ceilings will\nlower some. Guidance backed off on MVFR/IFR ceilings and \nvisibilities compared to the previous forecast, confining these\ncloser to the mountains. At this time, CHO has the highest\nchance of MVFR ceilings, which could last from around daybreak\nFriday into the afternoon before lifting.\n\nElsewhere, any temporary restrictions give way to VFR \nconditions later Friday morning as brief dry air moves into the\nregion. A leftover shower or thunderstorm is possible near KCHO\nFriday afternoon and evening as the front stalls nearby. Winds\nwill shift to the east or southeast by Friday evening. Sub-VFR\nceilings and visibilities may return northward, along with some\nrain, Friday night.\n\nAdditional scattered showers and thunderstorms are possible during \nthe afternoon/evening hours this weekend. This will lead to \ntemporary reductions amongst the terminals at times otherwise VFR \nconditions will prevail. South to southeast winds Saturday will \nswitch to the west and southwest winds Sunday at less than 15 kts.\nFair weather conditions expected during the first half of next week. \nWinds turn to the north and northwest Monday and Tuesday as VFR \nconditions continue.",
